WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.168
The method that we're looking for, for
distance based phylogeny construction,

00:00:05.168 --> 00:00:10.421
is called the "Neighbor-Joining Algorithm",
which was introduced in 1987.

00:00:10.421 --> 00:00:14.401
It's a result so
fundamental to bioinformatics, in fact,

00:00:14.401 --> 00:00:17.489
that it has been cited 30,000 times, and

00:00:17.489 --> 00:00:22.379
it's one of the top 20 most cited
papers over all scientific fields.

00:00:22.379 --> 00:00:24.753
So first, to see how this works,

00:00:24.753 --> 00:00:30.077
define the neighbor-joining matrix
of a given distance matrix, D,

00:00:30.077 --> 00:00:36.670
as the matrix D*, whose (i,j)-th entry is
given by the formula shown on the slide.

00:00:36.670 --> 00:00:39.313
So, let me give you an example.

00:00:39.313 --> 00:00:41.628
Here's the matrix that
we encountered before,

00:00:41.628 --> 00:00:46.585
where j and k corresponded to the minimum
element of the matrix, but j and

00:00:46.585 --> 00:00:50.254
k were not neighbors in
the tree that fit this matrix.

00:00:50.254 --> 00:00:55.148
The total distance of a leaf node is just
the sum of distances from this leaf to

00:00:55.148 --> 00:00:56.379
all other leaves.

00:00:56.379 --> 00:01:01.156
So for example,
the total distance of k in this matrix is

00:01:01.156 --> 00:01:04.420
21 plus 12 plus 13, or 46.

00:01:04.420 --> 00:01:09.722
We will then define D*(i,j) 
by taking n minus 2 times D(i,j) and

00:01:09.722 --> 00:01:16.046
then subtracting the total distance of i,
and subtracting the total distance of j.

00:01:16.046 --> 00:01:18.222
In this case, n, the number of leaves or

00:01:18.222 --> 00:01:21.420
the number of rows in our
distance matrix, is equal to 4.

00:01:21.420 --> 00:01:26.984
So D*(k,l)is equal 
to 2 times D(k,l),

00:01:26.984 --> 00:01:31.716
which is 13, minus 46, minus 48,

00:01:31.716 --> 00:01:38.419
which gives us a value of negative 68 for
D*(k,l).

00:01:38.419 --> 00:01:43.758
Now, if it's obvious to you that
defining this crazy matrix D* was

00:01:43.758 --> 00:01:49.836
going to be a productive endeavor, then
you are probably far smarter than I am.

00:01:49.836 --> 00:01:54.229
Like the Burrows-Wheeler transform,
which we encounter in another chapter,

00:01:54.229 --> 00:01:58.836
which makes matching multiple patterns
to a text a lightning-fast endeavor,

00:01:58.836 --> 00:02:01.531
I think this matrix D* is black magic.

00:02:01.531 --> 00:02:06.003
Whereas taking a minimum element
of the original distance matrix

00:02:06.003 --> 00:02:08.961
did not guarantee that we
were going to find neighbors,

00:02:08.961 --> 00:02:13.365
taking a minimum element of
the neighbor-joining matrix, D*,

00:02:13.365 --> 00:02:15.253
does find a pair of neighbors.

00:02:15.253 --> 00:02:19.211
You can find a proof of this
theorem in a detour in the text.

00:02:19.211 --> 00:02:20.795
It's not a short proof.

00:02:20.795 --> 00:02:24.396
In fact, although 
D* was introduced in 1987,

00:02:24.396 --> 00:02:28.170
it took a decade before a proof
of this theorem was found.

00:02:28.170 --> 00:02:32.679
Also, the Neighbor-Joining Theorem is
part of the reason why I led you down

00:02:32.679 --> 00:02:36.130
the wrong path in the first
place when we first attempted to

00:02:36.130 --> 00:02:38.962
construct a phylogeny
from a distance matrix.

00:02:38.962 --> 00:02:44.723
Now, if we use D* instead of D,
then we will be able to successfully

00:02:44.723 --> 00:02:50.212
identify a pair of neighbors from
a minimum element of the matrix.

00:02:50.212 --> 00:02:54.711
So let's walk through
the neighbor-joining algorithm.

00:02:54.711 --> 00:03:00.962
First, we construct the matrix D*, and
I'll move that up from the previous slide.

00:03:00.962 --> 00:03:07.170
So there are two pairs of leaves that
correspond to a minimum element of D*.

00:03:07.170 --> 00:03:12.836
i and j must be neighbors, and k and
l must be neighbors, as well.

00:03:12.836 --> 00:03:13.961
Let's work with say, i and j.

00:03:17.086 --> 00:03:20.874
We set delta(i,j)
equal to the total distance of i

00:03:20.874 --> 00:03:25.378
minus the total distance of j,
all divided by n - 2.

00:03:25.378 --> 00:03:30.028
So here when we plug those values in,
we see that delta(i,j)

00:03:30.028 --> 00:03:32.170
is going to be equal to 9.

00:03:32.170 --> 00:03:36.628
Now, you may remember before that we
had a formula for the LimbLength(i).

00:03:36.628 --> 00:03:41.503
The formula that neighbor-joining uses
is going to give the same result when

00:03:41.503 --> 00:03:44.503
the underlying distance
matrix D is additive.

00:03:44.503 --> 00:03:48.969
But this formula offers better results
when we deal with non-additive distance

00:03:48.969 --> 00:03:53.254
matrices, so that's why this formula
differs from the one that we had before.

00:03:53.254 --> 00:03:59.222
Here, LimbLength(i) is equal to one half
of the original distance matrix value D(i,j)

00:03:59.222 --> 00:04:00.795
plus delta(i,j),

00:04:00.795 --> 00:04:07.253
and LimbLength(j) is equal to (1/2)
D(i,j) - delta(i,j).

00:04:07.253 --> 00:04:12.349
So now that we have these limb lengths,
we can remove the i-th row and

00:04:12.349 --> 00:04:17.991
the j-th row, as well as the i-th
column and the j-th column from D, and

00:04:17.991 --> 00:04:22.905
then add a row that corresponds to
the parent node of i and j, or m, and

00:04:22.905 --> 00:04:27.461
then we denote this smaller 
matrix as D'.

00:04:29.296 --> 00:04:34.843
For each other leaf k, we then
compute the distance between k and

00:04:34.843 --> 00:04:40.193
this parent node, m, as the distance
from i to k, plus the distance

00:04:40.193 --> 00:04:45.461
from j to k, minus the distance
from i to j, all divided by 2.

00:04:45.461 --> 00:04:48.586
Does this formula look familiar?

00:04:48.586 --> 00:04:49.836
I hope so.

00:04:49.836 --> 00:04:54.048
It's the same formula that we
derived several sections ago,

00:04:54.048 --> 00:04:58.920
when we first attempted to reconstruct
a tree from an additive matrix.

00:04:58.920 --> 00:05:01.878
So, if we return to neighbor-joining,

00:05:01.878 --> 00:05:04.125
now that we have a smaller matrix,

00:05:04.125 --> 00:05:08.855
we can apply the neighbor-joining
algorithm recursively to D' and

00:05:08.855 --> 00:05:12.670
obtain the tree on the right
that fits this matrix D'.

00:05:12.670 --> 00:05:16.945
Once we have this tree,
we can simply re-attach the limbs of i and

00:05:16.945 --> 00:05:20.545
j using the limb lengths that
we previously computed.

00:05:22.836 --> 00:05:26.623
So here's the summary of
the neighbor-joining algorithm,

00:05:26.623 --> 00:05:31.208
which we apply recursively until we
obtain a 2 x 2 distance matrix when

00:05:31.208 --> 00:05:35.753
we form an edge, and then we retrace
our steps by adding limbs successively.

00:05:35.753 --> 00:05:39.586
Go ahead, try implementing it yourself.

00:05:39.586 --> 00:05:40.392
In particular, see what happens when

00:05:40.392 --> 00:05:44.539
the neighbor-joining algorithm 
is given a non-additive matrix,

00:05:44.539 --> 00:05:48.295
because we know what's going to happen
when it's given an additive matrix.

00:05:48.295 --> 00:05:54.128
The tree constructed by UPGMA on the same
non-additive matrix is shown on the right.

00:05:54.128 --> 00:05:58.124
How does the tree that
you create by running

00:05:58.124 --> 00:06:04.128
the neighbor-joining algorithm
differ from this UPGMA tree?

00:06:04.128 --> 00:06:08.186
Of course, we can also apply
neighbor-joining to the non-additive

00:06:08.186 --> 00:06:11.295
distance matrix that we
derive from coronaviruses.

00:06:11.295 --> 00:06:14.313
But we can also apply it
to a distance matrix that

00:06:14.313 --> 00:06:18.238
we obtain from pairwise alignment
of Spike proteins

00:06:18.238 --> 00:06:23.003
taken from different patients, as well
as from the palm civet coronavirus.

00:06:23.003 --> 00:06:27.707
When we do this, just from this
small sample of ten coronaviruses,

00:06:27.707 --> 00:06:33.068
we can see a very clear pattern of how
the disease was passed from the palm civet

00:06:33.068 --> 00:06:38.349
to residents in Southern China at the end
of 2002 and then how it spread to

00:06:38.349 --> 00:06:43.503
other other residents throughout
the beginning of early 2003.

00:06:43.503 --> 00:06:47.125
And then we see the cases from
the Metropol Hotel grouping together as

00:06:47.125 --> 00:06:48.505
the disease went global.

00:06:50.920 --> 00:06:54.576
It probably seems like we've come
a long way in our discussion

00:06:54.576 --> 00:06:58.878
of distance-based approaches for
constructing evolutionary trees.

00:06:58.878 --> 00:07:03.421
But we should point out that these
algorithms don't allow us to infer

00:07:03.421 --> 00:07:05.546
anything about the ancestors

00:07:05.546 --> 00:07:09.503
that are assigned to
the internal nodes of the tree.

00:07:09.503 --> 00:07:13.559
The reason why we can't infer
anything is because when we passed

00:07:13.559 --> 00:07:16.757
from a multiple alignment
to a distance matrix,

00:07:16.757 --> 00:07:21.128
we inherently lost information
about the sequences themselves.

00:07:21.128 --> 00:07:23.723
In order to say anything
about ancestral states,

00:07:23.723 --> 00:07:27.628
we're going to need an approach that
can analyze the alignment directly.

